WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
Welcome to a problem solving supplement for Genetics and Evolution.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:09.980
We will use pedigrees, linkage data and sequence evidence, then make each

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:10.800
assumption visible.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.660
A pedigree is a record of family relationships and a trait, not a diagnosis by

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:16.960
itself.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:23.340
Before calculating, state a model: for example, a fully penetrant autosomal

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.780
recessive allele with a rare disease allele.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.740
In that model, two unaffected carrier parents, A a and A a, have an affected

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.600
child a a with probability one quarter in each pregnancy.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:41.040
The chance is the same for every pregnancy; earlier children do not change it.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.560
An affected child with unaffected parents can fit this model, but other

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.880
explanations may also fit incomplete data.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.780
Track the evidence without claiming that one small pedigree proves the mode of

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:53.060
inheritance.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.808
Ask which observations distinguish the models, and which do not.

00:00:56.808 --> 00:00:59.608
Here is a common conditional probability trap.

00:01:00.208 --> 00:01:06.019
For two carrier parents, the four equally likely child outcomes are A A, A a,

00:01:06.019 --> 00:01:06.095
A

00:01:06.095 --> 00:01:07.568
a and a a.

00:01:07.568 --> 00:01:11.688
If a sibling is known to be unaffected, remove the affected a a outcome from

00:01:11.688 --> 00:01:12.248
the denominator.

00:01:13.128 --> 00:01:16.608
Two of the remaining three outcomes are carriers, so the conditional carrier

00:01:16.608 --> 00:01:19.188
probability is two thirds, not one half.

00:01:20.148 --> 00:01:23.468
Suppose that sibling has a child with an unrelated partner whose carrier

00:01:23.468 --> 00:01:25.688
probability is specified as one in fifty.

00:01:26.528 --> 00:01:30.648
Under the stated assumptions, the affected child probability is two thirds

00:01:30.648 --> 00:01:35.315
times one fiftieth times one quarter, which simplifies to one in three

00:01:35.315 --> 00:01:35.848
hundred.

00:01:35.848 --> 00:01:40.028
The partner risk is a question input; do not invent a population frequency.

00:01:40.808 --> 00:01:44.814
In every pedigree problem, write the conditioning information first, then

00:01:44.814 --> 00:01:45.088
count

00:01:45.088 --> 00:01:49.048
compatible genotypes, then multiply only independent transmission events.

00:01:49.272 --> 00:01:54.332
For an X linked recessive model, write the two sex chromosomes for each parent

00:01:54.332 --> 00:01:55.052
and child.

00:01:55.912 --> 00:01:59.952
A son receives his Y chromosome from his father and his single X chromosome

00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:00.692
from his mother.

00:02:01.452 --> 00:02:05.612
If the mother is a carrier, each son has a one half probability of receiving

00:02:05.612 --> 00:02:06.612
the variant X.

00:02:07.312 --> 00:02:11.972
An unaffected father does not transmit his X chromosome to a son, so father to

00:02:11.972 --> 00:02:14.352
son transmission is not expected under this model.

00:02:14.992 --> 00:02:19.872
A daughter receives her father's X chromosome; with an unaffected father, one

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:23.512
variant X from a carrier mother usually makes her a carrier rather than

00:02:23.512 --> 00:02:23.792
affected.

00:02:24.472 --> 00:02:28.672
These statements rely on the specified simple model, ordinary chromosome

00:02:28.672 --> 00:02:30.612
transmission and full penetrance.

00:02:31.412 --> 00:02:35.492
A pedigree can be small, and a rare trait may be absent by chance in one

00:02:35.492 --> 00:02:35.832
branch.

00:02:36.692 --> 00:02:40.672
Do not label a trait X linked only because more affected people happen to be

00:02:40.672 --> 00:02:40.852
male.

00:02:40.852 --> 00:02:43.232
Look for the complete transmission pattern.

00:02:43.488 --> 00:02:48.088
In a testcross, a double heterozygote is crossed with a homozygous recessive

00:02:48.088 --> 00:02:48.368
tester.

00:02:49.148 --> 00:02:53.548
If the tester contributes only recessive alleles, offspring classes reveal the

00:02:53.548 --> 00:02:55.068
gametes made by the heterozygote.

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:59.828
Suppose thirty six of two hundred forty offspring are recombinant across the

00:02:59.828 --> 00:03:00.328
two loci.

00:03:00.928 --> 00:03:05.455
The estimated recombination frequency is thirty six divided by two hundred

00:03:05.455 --> 00:03:07.008
forty, or fifteen percent.

00:03:07.008 --> 00:03:11.208
For a short interval, this is conventionally reported as about fifteen map

00:03:11.208 --> 00:03:13.108
units, or fifteen centimorgans.

00:03:13.808 --> 00:03:18.088
It is an estimate from sampled offspring, so a larger sample generally gives a

00:03:18.088 --> 00:03:19.108
more stable estimate.

00:03:20.408 --> 00:03:24.028
Recombination frequency cannot exceed fifty percent in the usual two locus

00:03:24.028 --> 00:03:28.066
scoring, because independent assortment also produces half recombinant

00:03:28.066 --> 00:03:28.528
classes.

00:03:29.628 --> 00:03:33.468
Multiple crossovers can restore parental allele combinations and make long

00:03:33.468 --> 00:03:34.928
physical distances appear smaller.

00:03:35.728 --> 00:03:39.605
Therefore, do not interpret a measured percentage as an exact physical

00:03:39.605 --> 00:03:40.048
distance

00:03:40.048 --> 00:03:41.088
across every interval.

00:03:41.352 --> 00:03:45.732
The same genotype letters can hide different chromosome arrangements.

00:03:46.612 --> 00:03:52.232
In coupling phase, write A B slash a b: the dominant alleles are together on

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:52.812
one homolog.

00:03:53.312 --> 00:03:58.892
In repulsion phase, write A b slash a B: each homolog carries one dominant and

00:03:58.892 --> 00:04:00.452
one recessive allele.

00:04:00.812 --> 00:04:05.192
A testcross identifies which offspring classes are common parental types and

00:04:05.192 --> 00:04:09.492
which are recombinant types, but phase must be recorded to interpret them.

00:04:09.972 --> 00:04:14.892
If coupling phase recombination frequency is twenty percent and reciprocal

00:04:14.892 --> 00:04:19.510
classes are equally frequent, the total parental fraction is eighty percent,

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:19.632
so

00:04:19.632 --> 00:04:22.332
each parental gamete class is forty percent.

00:04:22.332 --> 00:04:26.872
The total recombinant fraction is twenty percent, so each recombinant class is

00:04:26.872 --> 00:04:28.632
ten percent.

00:04:28.632 --> 00:04:31.772
Those paired expectations rely on the simple symmetric model.

00:04:32.592 --> 00:04:36.512
Do not call A B recombinant merely because it contains two capital letters.

00:04:36.512 --> 00:04:39.812
It is parental in coupling and recombinant in repulsion.

00:04:40.104 --> 00:04:44.984
Three point testcross data can reveal gene order and expose crossovers missed

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.264
by a two point estimate.

00:04:47.264 --> 00:04:48.384
Consider one thousand offspring.

00:04:49.144 --> 00:04:54.891
The two largest classes are A B C with four hundred ten and a b c with four

00:04:54.891 --> 00:04:55.904
hundred five.

00:04:55.904 --> 00:04:57.404
Treat these as the parental classes.

00:04:58.004 --> 00:05:03.084
The two smallest are A b C and a B c, one each, so they are the double

00:05:03.084 --> 00:05:03.624
crossovers.

00:05:04.644 --> 00:05:08.124
Compare A B C with A b C: only the B allele changes.

00:05:08.784 --> 00:05:12.284
Compare a b c with a B c: again only B changes.

00:05:13.004 --> 00:05:16.844
Therefore B is the middle locus, and the order is A, B, C.

00:05:16.844 --> 00:05:21.164
If the single crossover classes between A and B total one hundred forty five,

00:05:21.164 --> 00:05:24.824
that interval count becomes one hundred forty seven after adding both double

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:27.824
crossovers, or fourteen point seven percent.

00:05:27.824 --> 00:05:32.266
If the B to C single crossover classes total thirty eight, include the same

00:05:32.266 --> 00:05:32.444
two

00:05:32.444 --> 00:05:34.304
double crossovers to get four percent.

00:05:34.304 --> 00:05:37.304
Keep the intervals separate before summing map length.

00:05:37.704 --> 00:05:41.724
Sequence questions depend on strand labels and direction.

00:05:42.604 --> 00:05:48.416
If a coding DNA strand is written five prime A T G, G A A, T T

00:05:48.416 --> 00:05:54.124
T, T G A three prime, the messenger RNA has the same sequence with U

00:05:54.124 --> 00:05:59.859
in place of T: A U G, G A A, U U U,

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:01.344
U G A.

00:06:01.344 --> 00:06:04.844
Translation reads the RNA in groups of three from its five prime end.

00:06:05.804 --> 00:06:10.824
These codons specify methionine, glutamate, phenylalanine and then a stop

00:06:10.824 --> 00:06:11.124
signal.

00:06:12.284 --> 00:06:16.384
The template strand is complementary and antiparallel, so do not copy it

00:06:16.384 --> 00:06:17.944
directly as the coding sequence.

00:06:19.144 --> 00:06:23.461
A substitution may change one codon, create a stop, or be synonymous,

00:06:23.461 --> 00:06:24.024
depending

00:06:24.024 --> 00:06:24.644
on the code.

00:06:26.184 --> 00:06:29.884
Inserting or deleting one nucleotide usually shifts the reading frame for

00:06:29.884 --> 00:06:30.764
downstream codons.

00:06:31.644 --> 00:06:36.264
Inserting or deleting three nucleotides preserves the frame but can add or

00:06:36.264 --> 00:06:37.584
remove an amino acid.

00:06:38.204 --> 00:06:41.344
Always mark the strand, direction and frame before translating.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:46.100
Finish by separating a molecular regulation model from a population frequency

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:46.420
model.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:51.920
In the lac operon example, lactose derived allolactose binds the repressor and

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:53.600
reduces its attachment to the operator.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:58.580
This permits transcription when the other required conditions are present; it

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.620
does not guarantee the same expression in every cell context.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:06.640
For a separate Hardy–Weinberg calculation, suppose a fully recessive phenotype

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:10.200
occurs at frequency nine percent in a population at equilibrium.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:15.720
Under that model, q squared equals zero point zero nine, so q is zero point

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:17.980
three and p is zero point seven.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:23.540
The expected heterozygote frequency is two p q, or zero point four two.

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:27.480
This prediction assumes random mating at the locus and no relevant selection,

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:31.300
migration, mutation or drift that disrupts the model.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:35.700
A measured difference may reflect sampling, model violations or genotyping

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:36.000
error.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.760
It does not by itself identify which evolutionary force acted.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.420
State assumptions before interpreting a frequency.
